surfaces reduce CO2 with H2 to form formate, acetate and detectable pyruvate; this node encodes the pyruvate-forming branch as the balanced net C3 fixation step (Beyazay et al., 2023). The key limitation is concentration: reported pyruvate titers are far below those used in most downstream alanine experiments, so concentration or continuous flux is still an open problem."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "High-temperature CO coupling to pyruvate on Fe-Ni sulfides", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["FeS", "NiS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "470-600K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "FeS/NiS", "aqueous": true, "other": "CO-rich hydrothermal reactor; net stoichiometry shown, actual intermediate set is more complex."}, "mechanism": "Surface carbonyl coupling and insertion on Fe-Ni sulfides generate a C3 alpha-keto acid product branch.", "reasoning": "High-temperature Fe-S/CO chemistry can generate pyruvate under hydrothermal conditions; this JSON encodes the pyruvate-forming branch as the balanced net transformation 3 CO + 2 H2 -> pyruvate (Cody et al., 2000). The actual reactor chemistry is more complex than this net step, but the route is experimentally relevant as an upstream hydrothermal supplier of pyruvate."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Ni/H2 reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 to moderate", "catalyst": "Ni metal", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic water, pH about 7-9, NH4+/NH3 present."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate condenses with NH3 to an imine/iminium that is reduced by Ni-activated H2 to alanine.", "reasoning": "Ni catalyzes reductive amination of pyruvate with NH4+/NH3 under H2, giving alanine under mild aqueous conditions and fitting H2-rich serpentinization scenarios (Kaur et al., 2024; Kelley et al., 2005). Because all reagents are achiral, this route is represented as forming DL-alanine rather than L-alanine."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) oxyhydroxide reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) oxyhydroxide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353K", "pressure": "1 atm to moderate", "catalyst": "mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) oxyhydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline pH 9-10; batch wet-sediment residence times favored over flow-through chimney conditions."}, "mechanism": "An NH3-derived imine/iminium on pyruvate is reduced at a partially oxidized iron surface.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. showed strong alanine selectivity from pyruvate + ammonia with mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) oxyhydroxides at alkaline pH and 50-70 C, with maxima near a 1:1 redox ratio (Barge et al., 2019). The experiments use mineral redox equivalents rather than dissolved H2, so H2 here is an explicit mass-balanced bookkeeping reductant while the agent field preserves the experimentally supported mineral context. Their chimney-flow analog did not yield alanine, so long residence time is likely important."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Fresh FeS/Fe(OH)2-promoted reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["fresh FeS", "Fe(OH)2"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348K", "pressure": "1 atm to moderate", "catalyst": "fresh FeS/Fe(OH)2", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anaerobic, pH about 8.8-9, freshly precipitated mineral phase used in large excess."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate first forms an imine/iminium with NH3, then undergoes two-electron reduction mediated by fresh iron sulfide/hydroxide phases.", "reasoning": "Fresh FeS or Fe(OH)2 promotes reductive amination of pyruvate to alanine near pH 8.8-9, with reported alanine maxima around 52 mol% under optimized conditions (Huber & Wachtershauser, 2003). As in rxn_004, the real system uses a freshly precipitated mineral in large excess rather than free H2; the present stoichiometry is a balanced net representation of the demonstrated transformation. Keefe et al. (1995) cautioned that the wider FeS/CO2 autotrophic network is not thereby established."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Transition-metal sulfide vent-fluid alanine branch from pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["transition-metal sulfide minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-383K", "pressure": "moderate to high", "catalyst": "transition-metal sulfide mineral", "aqueous": true, "other": "Vent-fluid analog with NH4+, H2, H2S and CO2; strong competition from side reactions."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate enters a mineral-controlled vent-fluid network in which imine formation and reduction can yield alanine.", "reasoning": "Novikov and Copley mapped pyruvate reaction landscapes over transition-metal sulfides in vent-fluid analogs containing ammonium, H2, H2S and CO2 and observed alanine among the products (Novikov & Copley, 2013). This makes a hydrothermal sulfide-mediated alanine branch plausible, but competing sinks frequently dominated, so selectivity depends strongly on mineral identity and fluid composition."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "NiS-catalyzed hydration of acetylene to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["NiS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "375-380K", "pressure": "moderate, autogenous", "catalyst": "NiS", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline hydrothermal water; acetaldehyde formation strongly exceeds catalyst-free control."}, "mechanism": "NiS promotes hydration of acetylene to vinyl alcohol, followed by tautomerization to acetaldehyde.", "reasoning": "NiS catalyzes hydration of acetylene to acetaldehyde in water at about 105 C, providing a direct hydrothermal feeder into alanine Strecker chemistry (Communications Chemistry, 2023). The same study showed that downstream alanine formation remained low-yield and required added cyanide/ammonium, so this step is best treated as an upstream aldehyde source rather than a complete high-yield route by itself."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Spark-discharge formation of HCN and acetaldehyde from reduced gases", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_009", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K bulk; local discharge much hotter", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Lightning or plasma in a reduced volcanic plume or other localized reducing atmosphere."}, "mechanism": "Radical discharge chemistry fragments and recombines CH4/NH3/H2O into HCN, aldehydes and H2.", "reasoning": "Electrical discharge in reduced CH4/NH3/H2O mixtures generates HCN and aldehydes, and Miller identified these as the key precursors feeding aminonitrile chemistry to alanine (Miller, 1955; Miller, 1957). Johnson et al. (2008) and Parker et al. (2011) support localized volcanic-plume/H2S-rich variants, whereas Trail et al. (2011) argues against a persistently global strongly reducing atmosphere; this node is therefore best viewed as a local or episodic surface source."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Classical Strecker synthesis of alaninonitrile from acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_006", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Neutral to mildly alkaline aqueous pool; NH3/HCN ratio affects aminonitrile versus cyanohydrin branching."}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde reacts with NH3 to an imine/aminoalcohol manifold that is trapped by cyanide as 2-aminopropanenitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the classical Strecker hub for alanine: acetaldehyde, NH3 and HCN converge to 2-aminopropanenitrile as the balanced net transformation (Miller, 1957). Product partitioning is pH- and NH3/HCN-dependent because low-pH conditions favor lactonitrile/cyanohydrin chemistry rather than the aminonitrile branch, a point reinforced by the 2023 NiS-acetylene study."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrolysis of alaninonitrile to DL-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Prolonged aqueous hydrolysis; balance requires 2 H2O input and releases NH3."}, "mechanism": "The nitrile hydrolyzes through amide-like intermediates to the amino acid, regenerating ammonia.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of alaninonitrile to alanine is the standard terminal Strecker step; balance requires 2 H2O as input and NH3 is released (Miller, 1957). With achiral feedstocks, the expected product is racemic or near-racemic alanine, not specifically the L enantiomer."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Partition of DL-alanine into L- and D-enriched pools", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["mineral interfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-330K cycling", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "mineral interface (speculative)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Repeated dissolution-crystallization or adsorption-desorption at phase boundaries; outputs idealize separated enantiomer-enriched pools."}, "mechanism": "Physical resolution rather than covalent synthesis: selective adsorption/crystallization partitions a racemate into different enantiomeric pools.", "reasoning": "This step is explicitly speculative and represents physical resolution, not demonstrated direct asymmetric synthesis. The provided literature strongly supports abiotic DL-alanine but not a general prebiotic route to