", "description": "CO-based hydrothermal pyruvate formation feeds the mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxide alanine route. This combines a less direct upstream pyruvate source with one of the strongest downstream batch alanine-forming systems.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "CO carbonylation plus meteoritic Fe/Ni amination", "description": "CO-derived pyruvate is reduced and aminated on a Ni-rich meteoritic alloy under H2. The chemistry is plausible in outline but inherits the lower-confidence schematic CO-to-pyruvate step and still needs the shared L-enrichment edge.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "All-surface discharge Strecker route", "description": "Local reduced discharge chemistry supplies both HCN and acetaldehyde from methane-based feedstocks; classical Strecker formation of alaninonitrile followed by hydrolysis gives the alanine pool. This is chemically one of the best-supported abiotic routes, but only to a non-L-specific alanine pool.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Discharge HCN plus NiS-acetylene Strecker route", "description": "HCN is supplied by surface discharge chemistry, while acetaldehyde is supplied by NiS-catalyzed hydrothermal acetylene hydration; their convergence in aqueous Strecker chemistry gives alaninonitrile and then the alanine pool. This mixed-source path is experimentally grounded but low-yield and requires transport plus pH sequencing.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_009", "mol_012"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_003", "i